WEBVTT

1
00:00:07.706 --> 00:00:11.546
BEN: Hi there, and welcome to this week's People in the News ! Today we have a

2
00:00:11.547 --> 00:00:19.283
very special person to introduce to you—Barack Obama, the 44th president of the United States. He made

3
00:00:19.284 --> 00:00:24.708
history in 2008 by becoming America’s first African-American president. OLIVIA: He sure did, Ben. But Barack was making

4
00:00:24.709 --> 00:00:33.844
news long before that, as an attorney, community activist, writer, and senator. He was born in Hawaii

5
00:00:33.845 --> 00:00:43.449
in 1961. His parents were quite young when they got married, and they divorced when Barack was

6
00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:47.359
just two years old. His father eventually returned to his native country of Kenya. Except for a

7
00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:53.659
brief visit in 1971, Barack never saw his father again. Around 1967 Barack and his mother moved to

8
00:00:53.660 --> 00:01:00.182
Indonesia with his mother’s new husband. Life in Indonesia was very different from life in Hawaii. Barack

9
00:01:00.183 --> 00:01:04.337
was astonished by the amount of poverty in the country. The sight of starving children and beggars

10
00:01:04.338 --> 00:01:08.334
had a huge impact on Barack, and he thought it was unfair that people had to live

11
00:01:08.335 --> 00:01:17.653
under such terrible conditions. Barack's mother believed he'd get a better education in America, so she sent him

12
00:01:17.654 --> 00:01:26.400
back to Hawaii when he was ten years old to live with his grandparents. At first it

13
00:01:26.401 --> 00:01:30.817
was difficult for Barack to fit in with the other kids. He was one of the only

14
00:01:30.818 --> 00:01:38.976
African-American students in the school, and he’d just spent the last four years living in another country.

15
00:01:38.977 --> 00:01:45.484
Barack was a good student, but it wasn't until college that Barack became very serious about his

16
00:01:45.485 --> 00:01:49.798
studies. He often skipped going out with friends so that he could study instead. He also started reading

17
00:01:49.799 --> 00:01:58.696
about extreme poverty and human rights abuses, and grew determined to find a way to help fix

18
00:01:58.697 --> 00:02:03.043
these problems. After college Barack worked for a while as a business writer. He was earning a

19
00:02:03.044 --> 00:02:09.858
good salary, but he felt that something was missing from his life—he wasn’t helping anyone. So he

20
00:02:09.859 --> 00:02:16.007
quit his job and moved to Chicago, Illinois, to work as a community organizer. Now Barack's job was

21
00:02:16.008 --> 00:02:21.601
to help people who lived in one of Chicago's poorest neighborhoods. He spent long days walking around,

22
00:02:21.602 --> 00:02:28.434
getting to know people and seeing many of the problems they faced. With Barack's encouragement and assistance,

23
00:02:28.435 --> 00:02:35.194
some residents were able to pressure city officials to make improvements such as fixing roads, repairing plumbing,

24
00:02:35.195 --> 00:02:40.756
and removing asbestos from homes. Barack believed he could help people more if he had a law degree,

25
00:02:40.757 --> 00:02:47.843
so he entered the prestigious Harvard Law School. While there, he was elected president of the Harvard

26
00:02:47.844 --> 00:02:54.993
Law Review , a very important academic publication. This position would have guaranteed Barack a high-paying job

27
00:02:54.994 --> 00:03:00.388
in a big law firm when he graduated, but he didn't care about making a lot of

28
00:03:00.389 --> 00:03:05.814
money; he was still committed to public service. When he graduated from Harvard Law School in 1992, Barack

29
00:03:05.815 --> 00:03:16.557
returned to Chicago to teach at the University of Chicago Law School and work as a civil

30
00:03:16.558 --> 00:03:20.299
rights attorney. BEN: Why did Barack eventually get involved in politics? OLIVIA: He felt he could do

31
00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:26.058
more for people if he was helping to write the laws that affected them. Barack decided to

32
00:03:26.059 --> 00:03:32.462
run for a seat in the Illinois State Senate and was elected in 1996. As a state

33
00:03:32.463 --> 00:03:37.380
senator, he supported many laws that helped poor families. He also worked to expand health care for children,

34
00:03:37.381 --> 00:03:46.120
create tax breaks for low-income workers, and improve working conditions for many people. He helped to pass

35
00:03:46.121 --> 00:03:52.451
laws that protected individuals’ rights, and he fought corruption in the government. Barack knew that in order

36
00:03:52.452 --> 00:03:59.935
to make progress in the Senate, it was often necessary to compromise with others who held different

37
00:03:59.936 --> 00:04:05.354
views. He became known for his willingness to listen to and respect others' opinions. His success as a

38
00:04:05.355 --> 00:04:12.750
state politician gave him the confidence to run for a seat in the House of Representatives in

39
00:04:12.751 --> 00:04:15.199
2000. BEN: Did Barack win that election? OLIVIA: No, but he won four years later when he

40
00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:20.426
was elected to the U.S. Senate. As a senator Barack continued working on things to help the

41
00:04:20.427 --> 00:04:25.017
poor. He lobbied for tax breaks for low-income citizens, energy independence, and improved education. He also helped write

42
00:04:25.018 --> 00:04:32.073
a law that provided more government accountability. But he was the newest senator, which meant he had

43
00:04:32.074 --> 00:04:36.347
the least seniority, and he didn’t get much respect from other senators. Even though he played a

44
00:04:36.348 --> 00:04:44.308
minor role in the Senate, Barack was becoming very popular with the American public. He had delivered

45
00:04:44.309 --> 00:04:51.228
an important speech at the 2004 Democratic Convention, and he was the author of two best-selling books.

46
00:04:51.229 --> 00:04:59.149
His first book, Dreams from My Father , was a memoir, and his second, The Audacity of Hope

47
00:04:59.150 --> 00:05:09.889
, outlined his political beliefs and vision for America. As his popularity spread, other candidates started asking

48
00:05:09.890 --> 00:05:19.774
him for help with raising campaign money and invited him to speak at rallies across the country.

49
00:05:19.775 --> 00:05:27.434
By now Barack started to think about running for president of the United States. He knew it

50
00:05:27.435 --> 00:05:35.618
wouldn't be an easy campaign. At the time, every American president had been white, and many people didn't

51
00:05:35.619 --> 00:05:39.947
think an African-American could get elected. But Barack was immensely popular with much of the American public.

52
00:05:39.948 --> 00:05:49.601
So in 2007 he announced that he was running for president, and on Election Day in 2008,

53
00:05:49.602 --> 00:05:59.944
a record number of people turned out to vote. The majority of those people voted for Barack.

54
00:05:59.945 --> 00:06:04.655
Late that night, it was determined that America had elected its first African-American president! In 2012 Barack was

55
00:06:04.656 --> 00:06:10.344
elected to a second term. BEN: Thank you, Olivia. Barack's election was certainly an important moment in

56
00:06:10.345 --> 00:06:18.011
American history. See you soon, Little Fox readers, for another edition of People in the News !

